See [asm_analysis.md](Ressources/asm_analysis.md) for the complete ASM analysis.

The ASM analysis shows that the program essentially behaves like this:

if (atoi(argv[1]) == 423)
{
    str = strdup("/bin/sh");

    args[0] = str;
    args[1] = NULL;

    gid = getegid();
    uid = geteuid();

    setresgid(gid, gid, gid);
    setresuid(uid, uid, uid);

    execv("/bin/sh", args);
}
else
{
    fwrite("No !\n", 1, 5, stderr);
}

We notice that this program when argv[1] is equal to 423 launches a shell with the level1 permissions

We can now simply call ./level0 423

then cat /home/user/level1/.pass to retrieve the flag.




